      },
      "text": "### Cambium and Vascular Reconnection"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-GRAFTING.md#012",
      "path": [
        "02-GRAFTING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:horticulture:02-grafting, git-history:horticulture:02-grafting]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "concepts": "[grafting]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:grafting",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[grafting]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Grafting and Budding Techniques",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nANATOMY OF A WOODY STEM (cross-section):\n  Bark (outermost)\n  Phloem (inner bark) — carries sugars down from leaves\n  CAMBIUM (1–2 cell layer thick) — produces new phloem and xylem\n  Xylem (wood) — carries water + minerals up from roots\n  Pith (center)"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-GRAFTING.md#013",
      "path": [
        "02-GRAFTING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:horticulture:02-grafting, git-history:horticulture:02-grafting]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "concepts": "[grafting]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:grafting",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[grafting]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Grafting and Budding Techniques",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "GRAFT UNION FORMATION (3–6 weeks):\n  Step 1 (Days 1–5): Cut surfaces dry; cells die at margins.\n  Step 2 (Days 3–10): Surviving cambial cells produce callus\n    (undifferentiated parenchyma cells) that fill the graft interface.\n  Step 3 (Days 7–21): Callus cells from both scion and rootstock\n    intermingle and differentiate → new vascular connections form.\n  Step 4 (Days 21–42): Continuous vascular tissue crosses the union.\n    Phloem reconnects first (sugars can now flow).\n    Xylem reconnects (water and minerals can now flow).\n  Step 5: Scion begins independent growth."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-GRAFTING.md#014",
      "path": [
        "02-GRAFTING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:horticulture:02-grafting, git-history:horticulture:02-grafting]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "concepts": "[grafting]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:grafting",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[grafting]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Grafting and Budding Techniques",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "REQUIREMENTS FOR SUCCESS:\n  1. Cambium alignment: the key. Cells that are not in contact cannot unite.\n  2. Wound excluded from air and moisture loss (wax, grafting tape, parafilm).\n  3. Stable connection: any movement breaks forming callus bridges → failure.\n  4. Compatible species pairing (see incompatibility below).\n  5. Temperature: 15–25°C optimal for callus formation.\n```"
    },
