      "text": "## Rootstocks: What They Control"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-GRAFTING.md#017",
      "path": [
        "02-GRAFTING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:horticulture:02-grafting, git-history:horticulture:02-grafting]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "concepts": "[grafting]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:grafting",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[grafting]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Grafting and Budding Techniques",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The rootstock's root system connects to the soil; its vascular system feeds the canopy. This gives rootstocks profound influence over the whole tree:"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-GRAFTING.md#018",
      "path": [
        "02-GRAFTING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:horticulture:02-grafting, git-history:horticulture:02-grafting]",
        "canonical_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "concepts": "[grafting]",
        "current_path": "horticulture/02-GRAFTING.md",
        "id": "maxim:horticulture:grafting",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "horticulture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[grafting]",
        "section": "horticulture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Grafting and Budding Techniques",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nROOTSTOCK EFFECTS:\n  +------------------+------------------------------------------+\n  | SIZE CONTROL     | Dwarfing rootstocks produce smaller trees|\n  |                  | by restricting water/nutrient delivery   |\n  |                  | and through graft union restriction.     |\n  +------------------+------------------------------------------+\n  | PRECOCITY        | Dwarfing rootstocks induce earlier       |\n  |                  | flowering: typically 1–2 years earlier.  |\n  |                  | Important commercially: capital costs    |\n  |                  | until first harvest are minimized.       |\n  +------------------+------------------------------------------+\n  | SOIL ADAPTATION  | Different rootstocks tolerate: wet soils,|\n  |                  | dry soils, heavy clay, calcareous soils, |\n  |                  | cold soils (varies by species).          |\n  +------------------+------------------------------------------+\n  | DISEASE RESIST.  | Phytophthora crown rot resistance,      |\n  |                  | Replant disease tolerance, Fire blight  |\n  |                  | resistance (some apple rootstocks).      |\n  +------------------+------------------------------------------+\n  | FRUIT QUALITY    | Indirect: tree vigor affects fruit size, |\n  |                  | color development, sugar content.        |\n  |                  | High-vigor rootstocks → large trees →    |\n  |                  | excess shade → poor color development.   |\n  +------------------+------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
